item.text:
**Reused evidence**
No prior evidence items materially apply to 5B, so nothing was reused from earlier chapters.

**New evidence**
Accepted source-claim units are below.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Deep Structural Reforms Guided by Evidence Are Urgently Needed to Lift Millions of Nigerians out of Poverty, says New World Bank Report","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-03-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/03/21/afw-deep-structural-reforms-guided-by-evidence-are-urgently-needed-to-lift-millions-of-nigerians-out-of-poverty","claim":"The World Bank says as many as 4 in 10 Nigerians lived below the national poverty line, poverty was especially severe in the north, and pro-poor reform required fiscal space, including fuel-subsidy reform and stronger social protection.","locator":"press release body, poverty profile and reform paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release summarizing the report's findings and policy recommendations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Targets the poverty and policy baseline shaping 2022 welfare conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Not a personal act by Buhari alone; it describes country conditions and policy options during his administration.","contrary_evidence":["The report is based on prior surveys, so it does not by itself prove 2022 implementation effects."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"To Promote Inclusive and Sustainable Development, Nigeria Needs to Fix its Public Finances","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-11-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/11/21/to-promote-inclusive-and-sustainable-development-nigeria-needs-to-fix-its-public-finances","claim":"The World Bank says Nigeria financed inefficient and regressive petrol, electricity, and foreign-exchange subsidies that benefited mainly wealthy households, cost more than education, health, and social protection spending in 2021, and crowded out pro-poor programs while revenue and spending stayed very low.","locator":"press release description and closing quote paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release on the public-finance review with direct fiscal and distributional claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the 2022 fiscal structure and policy choices available to Buhari.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Buhari's fiscal authority, but also reflects inherited revenue structure and sectoral leakages.","contrary_evidence":["The review evaluates a multi-year fiscal structure, so it is not a clean measure of one-year personal performance."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"A 'Business Unusual' approach, even more urgently needed to reduce inflation, lessen fiscal pressures, and attract investment for jobs","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-14","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/14/a-business-unusual-approach-even-more-urgently-needed-to-reduce-inflation-lessen-fiscal-pressures-and-attract-investment","claim":"The World Bank said gasoline subsidies would cost Nigeria roughly 5 trillion naira in 2022, mainly benefit richer households, and leave the country unable to benefit fiscally from high oil prices unless it adopted a single market-responsive exchange rate, removed the subsidy, and redirected resources toward investment and jobs.","locator":"press release body, subsidy and policy-priority paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank policy note released during 2022 with explicit estimates and reform prescriptions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 macro-policy context for Buhari's administration.","ruler_attribution":"Administration-level attribution is fair for fiscal and exchange-rate choices, but oil prices and oil theft were not fully under Buhari's control.","contrary_evidence":["The report is advisory and does not itself prove the government implemented the recommended reforms in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"To reduce its vulnerability to crisis and rise to its potential, Nigeria has urgent choices to make","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-15","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/15/to-reduce-its-vulnerability-to-crisis-and-rise-to-its-potential-nigeria-has-urgent-choices-to-make","claim":"By late 2022 the World Bank said Nigeria's economy was still held back by business-as-usual management, inflation had surged to 21.1 percent, as many as five million more Nigerians had been pushed into poverty, and the petrol subsidy was likely to exceed five trillion naira, while reforms were needed to restore macro stability, broaden private investment, and expand social protection.","locator":"press release body, opening summary and reform paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank Nigeria Development Update press release with direct 2022 welfare and macro claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports late-2022 conditions under Buhari's final year.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Buhari's administration because the report ties the problems to fiscal and exchange-rate policy, but external shocks also matter.","contrary_evidence":["The report also acknowledges external oil-price conditions, so not all deterioration is ruler-controlled."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.4","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria: Staff Concluding Statement of the 2022 Article IV Mission","publisher":"IMF","publication_date":"2022-11-18","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/18/nigeria-staff-concluding-statement-of-the-2022-article-iv-mission","claim":"The IMF said higher oil prices created an opportunity to build fiscal space and FX reserves that was not harnessed, that fuel subsidies were a formidable drain on fiscal revenues, and that Nigeria needed tighter policy, stronger revenue mobilization, exchange-rate reform, and governance fixes including action on oil theft and leakages.","locator":"mission statement paragraphs 1, 4, 5, 6, and 10","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF mission statement written during the target year, with explicit fiscal and exchange-rate judgments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy assessment of the Buhari administration.","ruler_attribution":"Administration-level attribution is fair for fiscal, exchange-rate, and governance choices, though oil prices, theft, and security conditions are only partly attributable to Buhari.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF notes positive growth in services and agriculture, so the economy was not uniformly contracting."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigerian Gross Domestic Product Report (Q3, Q4 2022)","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics","publication_date":"2023-02-22","url":"https://www.nigerianstat.gov.ng/elibrary/read/1241328","claim":"Nigeria's real GDP grew 3.52 percent year-on-year in Q4 2022 and 3.10 percent for the year, with services driving growth while agriculture was hampered by severe floods and industry remained weak.","locator":"summary section and Q4 overview","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official national statistics report on target-period output, with clear quarterly and annual figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Reports 2022 economic performance directly.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly outcome/context rather than a pure ruler choice; agriculture and floods were significant external constraints, but macro management still matters.","contrary_evidence":["Positive overall GDP growth coexisted with weak household welfare and sectoral fragility."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CPI and Inflation Report December 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics","publication_date":"2023-01-16","url":"https://www.nigerianstat.gov.ng/elibrary/read/1241274","claim":"The headline inflation rate in December 2022 was 21.34 percent, 5.72 percentage points above December 2021, confirming a sharp deterioration in price stability by year-end.","locator":"Executive Summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official monthly inflation report with exact target-year price data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures December 2022 macro conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Mainly an outcome measure; monetary and fiscal policy are only partly attributable to Buhari through appointments and budget authority.","contrary_evidence":["The report is descriptive and does not isolate policy causation."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Monetary Policy Decisions","publisher":"Central Bank of Nigeria","publication_date":"2022-11-22","url":"https://www.cbn.gov.ng/MonetaryPolicy/decisions.html","claim":"The CBN's 2022 MPC decisions show a tightening cycle, with the Monetary Policy Rate raised from 11.5 percent to 13.0, then 14.0, 15.5, and 16.5 percent over the year.","locator":"2022 decision entries on the page for May, July, September, and November 2022","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official central-bank policy record listing the 2022 rate decisions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 monetary-policy record.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect and partial; Buhari did not set rates personally, but he appointed the CBN leadership and oversaw the fiscal environment.","contrary_evidence":["The same CBN record implies inflation and exchange-rate pressure were already severe, so tightening was reactive as well as corrective."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari Signs N17.127tr 2022 Budget Into Law, Directs MDAs to Commence Early Preparation of 2023 Transition Budget","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2021-12-31","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-signs-n17-127tr-2022-budget-into-law-directs-mdas-to-commence-early-preparation-of-2023-transition-budget/","claim":"Buhari signed the 2022 Appropriation Bill and 2021 Finance Bill into law, restoring a predictable January-to-December fiscal year, while asking for amendment and virement because the National Assembly had altered many projects and allocations.","locator":"opening paragraphs and presidential remarks","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official record of the president's fiscal act and stated concerns about budget changes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 enactment that governed the 2022 fiscal framework.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler action, though the resulting budget also reflects National Assembly amendments and inherited fiscal constraints.","contrary_evidence":["The president said the enacted budget still needed amendment/virement, suggesting implementation friction rather than a clean policy win."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Budget Speech: Budget of Economic Growth and Sustainability – President Buhari","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2021-10-07","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/2022-budget-speech-budget-of-economic-growth-and-sustainability-president-buhari/","claim":"Buhari framed the 2022 budget around conservative oil-price and exchange-rate assumptions, projected 4.2 percent growth and 13 percent inflation, and said the budget aimed at diversification, infrastructure, poverty reduction, and gender-responsive budgeting.","locator":"parameters/fiscal assumptions and spending-priority sections","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official budget speech directly states the administration's intended priorities, though it is self-presentational.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 policy proposal that set the 2022 budget agenda.","ruler_attribution":"Direct to Buhari as budget proposer, but it is intent rather than implementation.","contrary_evidence":["The budget speech sets intentions; it does not establish whether the promised outcomes materialized in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari Signs Three Bills Into Law","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-05-12","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-signs-three-bills-into-law/","claim":"Buhari signed three bills on money laundering, terrorism prevention, and proceeds of crime, saying they were part of the administration's fight against corruption and illicit financing.","locator":"opening paragraph and presidential remarks on the new laws","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official act with clear statutory content and anti-corruption purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and relevant to governance conditions affecting economic performance.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler action.","contrary_evidence":["Passing laws does not itself prove enforcement or reduced corruption in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In Bid to Achieve Universal Coverage, President Buhari Signs National Health Authority Bill Into Law","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-05-19","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/in-bid-to-achieve-universal-coverage-president-buhari-signs-national-health-authority-bill-into-law/","claim":"Buhari signed the National Health Insurance Authority Bill and said a fund would be created to cover 83 million poor Nigerians who cannot afford premiums, with a vulnerable-group fund and data systems to support implementation.","locator":"opening paragraph and presidential remarks on the coverage fund","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official act with explicit welfare-targeting language and named coverage figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 social-policy action.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler action, though actual coverage depends on follow-through by health institutions and states.","contrary_evidence":["Implementation and funding were not demonstrated by the signing alone."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria Launches its Most Extensive National Measure of Multidimensional Poverty","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics","publication_date":"2022-11-17","url":"https://www.nigerianstat.gov.ng/news/78","claim":"The 2022 MPI survey found that 63 percent of Nigerians, about 133 million people, were multidimensionally poor; 65 percent of the poor lived in the North and 72 percent of rural residents were poor.","locator":"press release highlights bullets","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official survey launch with concrete headline estimates and regional breakdowns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Measures 2022 poverty conditions and the distributional baseline during Buhari's final year.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly outcome/context rather than direct presidential choice; still relevant to whether the administration left households better off.","contrary_evidence":["The survey covers poverty conditions, not causal attribution to one ruler's policies."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari Approves Appointment of Semiu Adeyemi Adeniran as Statistician-General of the Federation and Chief Executive Officer of the National Bureau of Statistics","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics","publication_date":"2022-05","url":"https://nigerianstat.gov.ng/news/76","claim":"Buhari approved the appointment of Semiu Adeyemi Adeniran as NBS Statistician-General and CEO; the notice describes him as a professional statistician with more than three decades of experience and prior NBS leadership roles.","locator":"opening paragraph and biographical paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official appointment notice with a direct attribution and a specific technocratic résumé.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 appointment relevant to data capacity and evidence-based policy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler action, though one technocratic appointment does not prove the quality of the broader economic team.","contrary_evidence":["A single technocratic appointment does not offset other evidence of politicization in economic offices."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari's Speech at the Valedictory Session for Appointees Leaving the Cabinet to Pursue Political Ambition","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-05-13","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buharis-speech-at-the-valedictory-session-for-appointees-leaving-the-cabinet-to-pursue-political-ambition/","claim":"Buhari said cabinet selection had been careful to bring in the best and most competent people, that new members were added to inject fresh energies, skills, and ideas, and that replacements would be made without delay so governance would not suffer.","locator":"numbered paragraphs 2, 3, 4, 7, 9, 13, and 15","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official speech, but the competence claim is self-assessment rather than independent verification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 statement about cabinet composition and governance continuity.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler claim about his own appointments and cabinet management.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is self-justifying and does not independently prove competence or depoliticization."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ten Nigerian cabinet ministers resign to run in next year's election","publisher":"Reuters / Polity.org.za","publication_date":"2022-05-13","url":"https://www.polity.org.za/article/ten-nigerian-cabinet-ministers-resign-to-run-in-next-years-election-2022-05-13","claim":"Reuters reported that ten cabinet ministers resigned to pursue election bids and that the government had asked ministers, ambassadors, agency heads, and other political appointees including the central bank governor to resign if they planned to join the 2023 race.","locator":"opening paragraph and subsequent Reuters copy","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting, but it is indirect and partly about political appointee rules rather than economic performance itself.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence of politicization around key public offices.","ruler_attribution":"Directly traces to Buhari's directive, though the resignations reflect the broader election cycle and not economic policy alone.","contrary_evidence":["Buhari said replacements would be made quickly so governance would not suffer."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7","5B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across World Bank, IMF, NBS, CBN, State House, Reuters-syndicated reporting, and Transparency International.

Retained items were selected because they directly described 2022 policy, 2022 outcomes, or 2022 target-period decisions. I collapsed or rejected:
- Duplicate or mirrored Reuters stories on the same minister-resignation event.
- World Bank landing pages, blogs, and press releases that repeated the same poverty/public-finance findings without adding a new materially distinct claim.
- NBS microdata catalog pages and duplicate report listings when the report page already contained the needed 2022 figures.
- Background pages or later follow-ups that did not add new 2022-specific facts.
- Out-of-period material unless it explicitly reported 2022 data or a 2022 policy act that governed the 2022 fiscal year.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct evidence on how Buhari chose, evaluated, and empowered economic technocrats is still thin beyond the NBS appointment and his own cabinet speech.
- Budget execution, oil-revenue leakage audits, and social-protection rollout in 2022 would help separate announcement from implementation.
- More direct evidence on regional distributional effects, especially household welfare outside headline poverty and inflation series, would improve attribution.

Net read: the dossier shows some pro-stability and pro-data actions, but 2022 still ended with weak growth, high inflation, deep poverty, and highly regressive subsidy dynamics.
